{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: design note, pointing and claims in packages. Head 77d0c5520a3e081384efbd96e7717617f2983332, branch planner/pointing-claims-packages-20261027: one commit on Main 6f47bf98, and it merges cleanly onto the current Main 6499a12b. One new file: notes/2026-10-27-pointing-and-claims.md, 341 lines. Review request dbf9e2b5, checker promise 97293ad9, request 0e06e751, planner promise 6f798ee7. Artifacts: the notes directory (dfdc1ef2) and the note (6f61b081).\n\nRead order: the note in full; then the parts it amends or relies on: the self-contained applications note (sections 3 to 5, including 5.1, the presentation trust model), the packages note (sections 2 and 3), the capture note (section 2), and the landed R11 admission (packages/scope/src/applications/act-admission.ts at Main).\n\nConditions met:\n- Design note only, one new path. It changes no landed document.\n- Scene first, terms in order, no registry, decisions or open-points section in the body. The two open encodings (the agreed condition in 3.2, and the presented-entry form in section 4) are stated as open and assigned to a slice and a witness.\n- Plain English. Prose lines are at most 79 characters, except the title (80) and line 147, which is a code span. check-notes 6f47bf98..77d0c552 reports 0 warnings, git diff --check is clean, and check-commit-messages reports 0 flagged.\n- Consistent with the landed package design: the commons is a declared package, installed once for each room like the capture scope. Presentation stays outside the semantic id, as decision 1 of the self-contained applications note already says. The use-cases head (approved 5d7cc241) states the same rule.\n\nFour gaps block approval. Each is a sentence or a rule in the note plus a witness case; none changes the design's shape.\n\n1. Consent can be reused. Under consent \"claim\", an act's guards check that the claim is agreed, that its signed list holds the member written, that parties holds the act's signer, and that the claim's text digest equals the act's terms. Nothing stops the same agreed claim from being presented again, to a second act or a second item, that writes the same member into a slot. \"The contractor agrees to this change at this price\" would then bind the contractor to every later change order with the same words. Fix it with one of these, in the note:\n   - the claim names what it consents to (the target item, or the act and the item), and the guard compares it; or\n   - a consuming act records the claim as used, with a monotone one-use marker, like the certificate marker the R7 hardening landed (Q12).\n   Add the reuse case to W3: the same agreed claim, presented a second time, is refused with a named reason.\n\n2. Member-adopted presentations bypass operator trust. Section 5 lets anyone ship a presentation package for a semantic id, and lets a member adopt it with the commons act adopt. The host then serves that member's sessions with it, in the browser and over MCP. Today only a package its operator trusts is installed (self-contained applications note, section 5.1 and M1), and since R11 the release must also be in the Worker's APPLICATION_TRUST. A presentation carries ui:// resources and act descriptions; the descriptions alone can mislabel what an act does. State that the host serves an adopted presentation only when its release is in the operator's trust list, and checks this when each session starts (as R11 checks admission on each use). A member's adopt records the choice and grants nothing. Add to W4: adopting an untrusted presentation is recorded, but the session falls back to the room default, with a named reason.\n\n3. One member can exhaust the bounds. A thing has no final state and the live bound is 100, so 100 point acts by any one member stop pointing for the room for good. A proposed claim stays live until its proposer withdraws it or every party signs. A member can therefore open 100 claims that name a party who never signs, and stop all new claims. Section 10 calls these bounds to measure, but the failure is structural, not a question of size. Say how a room recovers: for example, a per-member share of each bound, an expiry for a proposed claim (to withdrawn), or an admin act that retires things. Add the exhaustion case to the PC1 witness.\n\n4. The commons is subject to R11. As a declared package, its release must be in every Worker's APPLICATION_TRUST, or every fresh point, say and claim act in every room is refused (application-revoked). Removing that one trust row would also withdraw pointing and claims from every room at once. State this, and choose between keeping the commons an installed, trusted package (and naming its trust row in the PC1 deployment) or making it a native platform definition pinned in the creation cohort. \"Installed at room creation by the host's default cohort\" mixes the two. Cohorts pin native versions today, and packages arrive through a Rules installation that fetches a release URI.\n\nThe encoding for every-party-signed: use a count, not a list comparison. Make parties unique at claim opening, and refuse a duplicate. sign already requires that the signer is in parties and not yet in signed. Together these keep signed a duplicate-free subset of parties, so \"every member of parties is in signed\" holds exactly when count(signed) equals count(parties). The sign effect is then one conditional: if count(signed) + 1 = count(parties), set agreed. It is bounded by 16, needs no set operator, and W2's cases (agreed in C's entry, B's second signature refused, a one-party close agreed at opening) fit it as they stand. For the presented-entry form in section 4, I would choose the second encoding (opening entry beside the agreeing entry). The opening entry carries the fixed parties and text. The guard then checks that both entries name the same claim item, and the one-use marker from point 1 can name that item.\n\nArchitecture: sound in shape. The commons gives every room a common way to point and to claim. Package structure enters only through a claim or a granted authority. Item ends are separated from party closings. That serves the cases the note names. The four points above are where its rules need to close.\n\nSecurity: points 1 to 3 are the findings. Locators are display data and unverified, and the note says so; statements are attributed entries; a one-party same, close or dispute claim changes no other item. Those hold.\n\nSimplification: acceptable. One package, two item types and eight acts. The consent rule adds three values on an existing slot rule, and the default (signer) refuses an unstated slot. The count encoding above keeps 3.2 to one conditional.\n\nVerdict: changes requested at exact head 77d0c552. A recut that adds the four rules and the witness cases is documents only and needs no gate. I will review it at its exact head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dfdc1ef28f493821391660dec5179660bb2c824b","binding":"assigned","head":"77d0c5520a3e081384efbd96e7717617f2983332","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:97293ad99074fab1f0a935bc036add69a2038897\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6f798ee7856d573469b23f1e863f393b8b4c678d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7097f15cb551542680da75442a4c967a899849ff","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}